#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064016 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064372 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a61a3e76-4936-5976-abf0-b9b485c4434d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Area Given the Rate of Change of the Side Length"}] :question/id #uuid "ec1aaa71-efe4-50fd-8552-bcc98abbd3a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-218" :question/problem "Each side of a square is decreasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{cm/min}.$ How fast is the area of the square decreasing when the sides are $10\\,\\text{cm}?$\n\n![](images/a0/a06eb0963dc6d01e45acb9349e5232ba70a11c98c99ee63c2ec0dae3d748f5ed.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} .\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA = x^2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is decreasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{cm/min},$ so we have $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} = -2.$ Also, when the sides are $10\\,\\text{cm},$ we have $x=10.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =(-2)\\cdot 2(10)=-40{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square decreases at a rate of $40\\,\\text{cm}^2/\\text{min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064017 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064373 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2edbb40a-1d59-5c75-bcc0-93584ec9c030" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for the Rate of Change of a Side Length Given the Rate of Change of the Area"}] :question/id #uuid "8780fe5c-94c5-5ac6-b6f6-6e86df29b416" :question/math-academy-id "e-219" :question/problem "The area of a square decreases at a rate of $20 \\text{ cm}^2\\text{/s}.$ If $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square respectively, then find an expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}$, where $t$ is the time in seconds." :question/worked-solution "This situation is different. Here, the *area* is changing at a constant rate,  and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/f4/f4125cebd7553bb8b106a8baff39a6e4a120b9d9e5cdf2c4c9c0a9a676558897.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA = x^2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-20\\text{ cm[math]^2[/math]/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-20 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{10}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064018 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064374 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e2af960-841b-56a9-bc7c-099bf82e0fe8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Area Given a Rate of Change of a Diagonal: Word Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "6264adc4-c5ab-5b84-b89f-27b4382baa0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-220" :question/problem "![](images/26/2677121139896da73eca5277c7c23f193467001eabba976cb48b2232f4bcbad6.png)\n\nIn a testing facility, a scientist is increasing the diagonals of a simple square at a rate of $12 \\text{ mm}\\text{/min}.$ At what rate, in squared millimeters per minute, is the area of the square increasing when the length of the diagonal is $6\\, \\text{mm}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{h^2}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ increases at a rate of $12\\,\\textrm{mm/min},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=6.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}A}{\\textrm d t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =12\\cdot 6=72{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064019 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064375 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1659900c-f6c4-5880-9d2a-4ee68c390432" :knowledge-point/title "Relating the Rate of Change of a Diagonal Length to Another Rate of Change"}] :question/id #uuid "7187c402-9949-5671-af33-8929bc5344a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-221" :question/problem "The sides of a square are increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the square is numerically equal to twice the rate of increase of the diagonal. What is the area of the square at that moment?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{h^2}{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing implicit differentiation and the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = 2\\dfrac{\\textrm d h}{\\textrm d t}$, so replacing $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm dt}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =h\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} & =h\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 2 & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at that particular moment, we have $h=2,$ and therefore the area of the square at that moment is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{2^2}{2} = 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064372 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a61a3e76-4936-5976-abf0-b9b485c4434d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Area Given the Rate of Change of the Side Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c35528d-6a69-5348-80f8-4fb876374bd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\text{ m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520826} {:answer/id #uuid "251d3cf9-4f92-5be8-aa35-c4057d43ad83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\text{ m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520843} {:answer/id #uuid "3be5b30f-4289-592e-8f61-90413aa1af64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5\\text{ m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520896} {:answer/id #uuid "3ddd82a8-41d8-5633-b335-922238ee66a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\text{ m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520901} {:answer/id #uuid "fa4a3b6b-fdc1-5d0c-89ef-a7234b4f419c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\text{ m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ddd82a8-41d8-5633-b335-922238ee66a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\text{ m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520901} :answer-field/id #uuid "b962a43a-d4a9-571b-a6b2-7e3f1cbc1ef3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8cec0878-9352-5be3-a087-f066a0b92d19" :question/math-academy-id "q-115924" :question/problem "The sides (in meters) of a square are decreasing. At a particular moment, the rate of decrease of the area of the square is numerically equal to ten times the rate of decrease of the sides. What is the length of the sides of the square at that moment?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=10\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ So, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 10\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}} & =2x\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 10 & =2x \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, at that particular moment we have $x=5\\text{ m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521497 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064375 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1659900c-f6c4-5880-9d2a-4ee68c390432" :knowledge-point/title "Relating the Rate of Change of a Diagonal Length to Another Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "131bb04e-bc65-52c2-a042-ead28c18bb28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\text{ feet}/\\text{hour}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520806} {:answer/id #uuid "357f7502-20f6-552a-bf7e-48abdb63a59e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\text{ feet}/\\text{hour}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520877} {:answer/id #uuid "5800a282-089f-5bee-b27e-6bfc03da7f2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\text{ feet}/\\text{hour}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520962} {:answer/id #uuid "58527ab4-efe9-53d9-9523-50427c75098c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\text{ feet}/\\text{hour}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520964} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba663cb-d89a-5b2b-94de-62b822f02bab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\text{ feet}/\\text{hour}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5800a282-089f-5bee-b27e-6bfc03da7f2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\text{ feet}/\\text{hour}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520962} :answer-field/id #uuid "dad03ac8-e6b0-55d6-a367-f056cb5c48e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521437}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5082ae1f-b15c-594a-83e6-841946d45040" :question/math-academy-id "q-116049" :question/problem "![](images/bb/bbd79b56420475595834e8cd67d4b9d3d5ca8a2e0fda399f49de1f4e9bb26c95.png)\n\nIf the diagonals of a square increase at a rate of $4\\sqrt{2}\\text{ feet}\\text{/hour}$, at what rate is the perimeter changing?" :question/worked-solution "Let $p$ be the perimeter of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $p$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\np=2\\sqrt{2}h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each diagonal of the square is increasing at a rate of $4\\sqrt{2}\\text{ feet}\\text{/hour},$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=4\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into the above expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =(4\\sqrt{2})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})=16\\text{ feet/hour}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the square increases at $16\\text{ feet}/\\text{hour}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521457 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064375 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1659900c-f6c4-5880-9d2a-4ee68c390432" :knowledge-point/title "Relating the Rate of Change of a Diagonal Length to Another Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24c81138-05fa-586e-96b9-07589b7d7c89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42\\text{ mm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520841} {:answer/id #uuid "2f5f17d2-7461-58c3-870a-3459da5761ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\text{ mm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520863} {:answer/id #uuid "7a1efe3d-09db-5caa-8705-9b5fa2d74f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\text{ mm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521038} {:answer/id #uuid "b2ae7e2c-192a-5e5d-af15-24061dc8c47e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22\\text{ mm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521163} {:answer/id #uuid "eb0ed7e5-09ed-5b08-ab64-ff34690c3ecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\text{ mm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a1efe3d-09db-5caa-8705-9b5fa2d74f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\text{ mm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521038} :answer-field/id #uuid "84dd87d0-922b-58de-8a95-b91eeb107edc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0f159c95-e1be-591f-b6cb-f9ebf9404573" :question/math-academy-id "q-116055" :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb40860015e5404f3680b22835149ddc63baf80cd8e1f34e30f936308c3221e0.png)\n\nIf the diagonals of a square decrease at a rate of $10\\sqrt{2}\\text{ mm}\\text{/min}$, at what rate is the perimeter decreasing?" :question/worked-solution "Let $p$ be the perimeter of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $p$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\np=2\\sqrt{2}h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each diagonal of the square is decreasing at a rate of $10\\sqrt{2}\\text{ mm}\\text{/min},$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-10\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into the above expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10\\sqrt{2})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})=-40\\text{ mm/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the square decreases at $40\\text{ mm}/\\text{min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064640 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064372 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a61a3e76-4936-5976-abf0-b9b485c4434d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Area Given the Rate of Change of the Side Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f08dcdb-af34-5a27-8c71-56b58794b8e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520905} {:answer/id #uuid "6240a033-4475-5d68-a24e-51e9eb3191cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520988} {:answer/id #uuid "635c658c-f5ae-5a89-ba69-4a562ce5f220" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520990} {:answer/id #uuid "8773b3bc-ffa1-58c8-9d06-4516207829f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521075} {:answer/id #uuid "954a78d3-b868-53eb-ad2d-108c5bf1d930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8773b3bc-ffa1-58c8-9d06-4516207829f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521075} :answer-field/id #uuid "eef0677f-b6e1-5448-a282-0aa8c9254820" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3d9b3637-d8c0-5460-ba5e-d5e1eb8257d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-24800" :question/problem "![](images/74/740d181b8e8eff44f0eb258c8b68f30d05f70445aefabd5c00fd68e570e825dd.png)\n\nThe sides of a square are increasing in length at a rate of $3\\text{ mm/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the square changing when the sides have a length of $12\\text{ mm}?$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is increasing at a rate of $3\\,\\text{mm/s},$ so we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3.$ Also, when the sides are $12\\,\\text{mm},$ we have $x=12.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =3\\cdot 2(12)=72{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square increases at $72\\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064375 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1659900c-f6c4-5880-9d2a-4ee68c390432" :knowledge-point/title "Relating the Rate of Change of a Diagonal Length to Another Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c44563a-1326-5d99-9881-696a2bb3906d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520784} {:answer/id #uuid "2186a87e-24dc-58c2-a7ab-6583096c289d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520833} {:answer/id #uuid "5eab803d-e2e7-5163-bf15-0810f1431535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520976} {:answer/id #uuid "ba4f4407-5a99-5b48-917e-de381c7c9cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521180} {:answer/id #uuid "ec960d32-a56e-5be1-a636-fdd4c8d72cf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521291}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5eab803d-e2e7-5163-bf15-0810f1431535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520976} :answer-field/id #uuid "48f0d76a-3c1e-522a-8915-c0902dc2770d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "08e98843-e2bc-5a10-8ff2-9ed917780859" :question/math-academy-id "q-24805" :question/problem "![](images/19/197557b866b4a4a1c2c151c29cc529e12a5048b941223fe8e2eb4a7c4959223e.png)\n\nThe diagonals of a square increase in length at a rate of $1.5\\text{ mm/min}.$ At what rate is the area of the square changing when the diagonals are $4\\,\\text{mm}$ long?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each diagonal of the square is increasing at a rate of $1.5\\,\\text{mm/min},$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=1.5.\n$$\n\nAlso, when the diagonals are $4\\,\\text{mm},$ we have $h=4.$ Substituting this information into the above expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=1.5\\cdot 4=6{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square increases at $6\\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521561 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064372 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a61a3e76-4936-5976-abf0-b9b485c4434d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Area Given the Rate of Change of the Side Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23286283-12a6-5c29-ac96-a63e013fea9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520836} {:answer/id #uuid "52280bf1-eb40-5698-9eab-29a3665e6262" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520950} {:answer/id #uuid "54f0f721-c507-5e47-bac7-834e87887094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520957} {:answer/id #uuid "84d32b58-5c12-5e75-9b6a-b4f0565bb964" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521067} {:answer/id #uuid "a3fe0de0-f7c7-5ab2-ac5b-527ba64fd164" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54f0f721-c507-5e47-bac7-834e87887094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520957} :answer-field/id #uuid "205c8531-1b66-5ba4-8976-46d4ed255c28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4eddc77-b22f-562d-ab08-be01c533b457" :question/math-academy-id "q-2773" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd63afc83917b365d1b91bf4b033be10b64c76a18e7aa16cb8019dc8ef197eb1.png)\n\nThe sides of a square increase in length at a rate of $6\\text{ cm/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the square increasing when the sides are $4\\text{ cm}$ long?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is increasing at a rate of $6\\,\\text{cm/s},$ so we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=6.$ Also, when the sides are $4\\,\\text{cm},$ we have $x=4.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =(6)\\cdot 2(4)=48{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square increases at $48\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064372 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a61a3e76-4936-5976-abf0-b9b485c4434d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Area Given the Rate of Change of the Side Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b7a1700-34a9-5048-a8fc-580d0f5f6abb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b7a1700-34a9-5048-a8fc-580d0f5f6abb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520857} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0cbefda-a203-5ea4-914e-7a55a9ace9c4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b66e27f7-fcba-5767-8cef-431ef74750b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-314491" :question/problem "![](images/e8/e8944ed32d3019a0d0d1ccebbc2f40c5248c221f3cddba237906db509fac0737.png)\n\nThe sides of a square are increasing in length at a rate of $8\\text{ ft/s}.$ At what rate is the area of ​​the square increasing when the side is $4\\,\\text{ft}$ long?\n\nThe area is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{ft}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is increasing at a rate of $8\\,\\text{ft/s},$ so we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=8.$ Also, when the sides are $4\\,\\text{ft},$ we have $x=4.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =8\\cdot 2(4) \\\\  & =64{\\text{ ft}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square increases at $64\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064375 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1659900c-f6c4-5880-9d2a-4ee68c390432" :knowledge-point/title "Relating the Rate of Change of a Diagonal Length to Another Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66ffdd0c-16fb-5f50-b5ba-1cb07f13ccce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372542}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66ffdd0c-16fb-5f50-b5ba-1cb07f13ccce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372542} :answer-field/id #uuid "6262049f-56fe-5753-8d3a-24cb4987048f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d06153ec-06de-5e08-a5e0-36bc3ef9e7e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-314495" :question/problem "The sides (in feet) of a square are increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the square is numerically equal to seven times the rate of increase of the diagonals. What is the length $h$ of the diagonal of the square at that moment?\n\n$h=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft}$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=7\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$, so replacing $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =h\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 7\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} & =h\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 7 & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at that particular moment, we have that the length of the diagonals of the square is $7\\,\\text{ ft}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521480 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064374 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e2af960-841b-56a9-bc7c-099bf82e0fe8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Area Given a Rate of Change of a Diagonal: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f798b7f-7d7a-5343-9738-1b34c019d8a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f798b7f-7d7a-5343-9738-1b34c019d8a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521089} :answer-field/id #uuid "3836a26d-16ce-5b20-91e1-5b41083d18ec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "37d3dfcf-127d-51c3-8809-c6a386eec680" :question/math-academy-id "q-314510" :question/problem "![](images/00/007ac521cd9bd97773aa18e243e788ce9eb473a65e3242022539266bc8f23ef4.png)\n\nA laser technician is reducing the diagonals of a square aperture at a rate of $2.4\\text{ mm}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the aperture decreasing when the diagonals are $12.5\\,\\text{mm}$ long each?\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe area is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ decreases at a rate of $2.4\\,\\text{mm/s},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-2.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=12.5.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-2.4\\cdot 12.5=-30{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the square aperture is decreasing at a rate of $30{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372890 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064372 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a61a3e76-4936-5976-abf0-b9b485c4434d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Area Given the Rate of Change of the Side Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a74594c-0ef1-5fc3-9a5b-718328e0c736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a74594c-0ef1-5fc3-9a5b-718328e0c736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372892} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbe1680e-65fa-58d0-9937-619069d11c05" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "326e805a-53b2-5770-bd3f-5b9abe674ca8" :question/math-academy-id "q-344438" :question/problem "The sides (in meters) of a square are decreasing. At a particular moment, the rate of decrease of the area of the square is numerically equal to twelve times the rate of decrease of the sides. What is the area $A$ of the square at that moment?\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{ m}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=12\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ So, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 12\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}} & =2x\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 12 & =2x \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, at that particular moment, we have $x=6\\text{ m}$ and the area of the square is\n\n$$\nA={6}^{2}=36{\\text{ m}}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521503 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064373 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2edbb40a-1d59-5c75-bcc0-93584ec9c030" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for the Rate of Change of a Side Length Given the Rate of Change of the Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17145308-c3c7-5435-ad19-e0ed2a06df6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17145308-c3c7-5435-ad19-e0ed2a06df6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520817} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6517593-c488-552c-8243-888093248acc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e8039e3-82ac-52bc-a02f-bab2dbe2062b" :question/math-academy-id "q-344456" :question/problem "The area of a square increases at a rate of $24{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$ Given that $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square, respectively, find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in minutes, as a function of $x.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate, and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/e2/e2b8bf4ff974ba499167a4349c894ea3c4c87cc14c731cf806e47d58c8c6e027.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=24\\text{ }{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{12}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521508 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064373 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2edbb40a-1d59-5c75-bcc0-93584ec9c030" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for the Rate of Change of a Side Length Given the Rate of Change of the Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "960848f1-cdc7-5fcf-a7f2-24297ee9fc40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "960848f1-cdc7-5fcf-a7f2-24297ee9fc40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521106} :answer-field/id #uuid "12122b0d-d6c1-5e9e-83ff-746168fe72f0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6378a8a6-ced0-5ab5-b937-c9000ce1db16" :question/math-academy-id "q-344457" :question/problem "The area of a square increases at a rate of $20{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$ Given that $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square, respectively, find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in seconds, as a function of $x.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate, and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/e2/e2b8bf4ff974ba499167a4349c894ea3c4c87cc14c731cf806e47d58c8c6e027.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=20\\text{ }{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{10}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372899 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064373 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2edbb40a-1d59-5c75-bcc0-93584ec9c030" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for the Rate of Change of a Side Length Given the Rate of Change of the Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "030b95f8-281c-522a-9c71-ee2a31868eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "030b95f8-281c-522a-9c71-ee2a31868eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372901} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab6ddcc7-548b-528c-b8e3-9ff38fb06abf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "175199e1-96a5-594b-b670-66eb928d5ac0" :question/math-academy-id "q-344507" :question/problem "The area of a square decreases at a rate of $30{\\text{ in}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$ Given that $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square, respectively, find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in minutes, as a function of $x.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate, and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/a4/a42b7ac5c9f5870d256b6474c9b95730d32184ba22ca80235c76ffe0ff346f31.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-30\\text{ }{\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-30 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{15}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064374 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e2af960-841b-56a9-bc7c-099bf82e0fe8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Area Given a Rate of Change of a Diagonal: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5be44bdb-8b94-5510-8afa-27e2682bad80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5be44bdb-8b94-5510-8afa-27e2682bad80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520971} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bc6154d-11f3-5894-9fac-15ff31065158" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dd3d0969-d794-5861-a620-0bc6a8d07956" :question/math-academy-id "q-344649" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cb048e7ac152ffbc740d611726871f395b4c6c4abcc324a4eed890e6116ec814.png)\n\nA manufacturing process gradually increases the size of a square opening by lengthening its diagonals at a rate of $1.6\\text{ mm}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the opening increasing when each diagonal measures $25\\,\\text{mm}?$\n\nThe area is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ increases at a rate of $1.6\\,\\text{mm/s},$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=1.6.\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=25.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =1.6\\cdot 25=40{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the square opening is increasing at a rate of \n$40{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500334 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064374 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e2af960-841b-56a9-bc7c-099bf82e0fe8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Area Given a Rate of Change of a Diagonal: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c482fa62-bdad-55bc-8f75-9c1122d494b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500052} {:answer/id #uuid "11f56a07-742f-503a-bfa2-c32cba6871e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11f56a07-742f-503a-bfa2-c32cba6871e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520802} :answer-field/id #uuid "540ea305-c7f1-5022-950d-9653c1a23171" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a2135fd1-33ca-5d7e-9987-278b24125900" :question/math-academy-id "q-344664" :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef685549b562c570914bc8b5b5066cb621e42c8536586b37467afc6c1eb6c194.png)\n\nAn adjustable square skylight is being expanded as part of a building renovation. The area of the skylight is increasing at a rate of $27{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate are the diagonals increasing when each diagonal measures $18\\,\\text{mm}?$\n\nThe diagonal is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} $\\text{mm/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the area of the square skylight, and $h$ be the length of its diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the relationship between $A$ and $h$ with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the area of the square is increasing at a rate of $27{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s},$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=27.\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the diagonals when the diagonal has length $h=18.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{18}\\cdot 27 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{27}{18} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =1.5\\text{ mm/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the diagonal of the skylight is increasing at a rate of \n$1.5\\text{ mm/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521530 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064374 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e2af960-841b-56a9-bc7c-099bf82e0fe8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Area Given a Rate of Change of a Diagonal: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61de9dc8-279b-5f59-904b-21e15c2aa717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61de9dc8-279b-5f59-904b-21e15c2aa717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520986} :answer-field/id #uuid "046001e6-52ad-564f-b292-d2455a9b9819" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521329}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9793c42f-eccf-5af0-a22e-1cfcdfee54a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-344674" :question/problem "![](images/34/34a41e39a36d3e073ef2ca4144bd346b340efa22a9da7d3e8a9df48d32d900b3.png)\n\nA square opening in a testing chamber is being reduced, causing the area of the opening to decrease at a rate of $49{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate are the diagonals decreasing when each diagonal measures $35\\,\\text{mm}?$\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe diagonal is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} $\\text{mm/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the area of the square opening, and $h$ be the length of its diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the relationship between $A$ and $h$ with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the area of the square is decreasing at a rate of $49{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s},$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-49.\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the diagonals when the diagonal has length $h=35.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{35}\\cdot (-49) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{49}{35} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.4\\text{ mm/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the diagonal of the opening is decreasing at a rate of \n$1.4\\text{ mm/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392642 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064375 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1659900c-f6c4-5880-9d2a-4ee68c390432" :knowledge-point/title "Relating the Rate of Change of a Diagonal Length to Another Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "797a4f0c-845d-5bd4-a00e-f4042c09b16b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.5\\text{ feet}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392644} {:answer/id #uuid "c6bd86db-1af1-529e-b7e3-3d8c2f9d1330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\text{ feet}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392645} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff2b416-758c-5d4f-944b-1eac169b1cce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\text{ feet}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392646} {:answer/id #uuid "bb340c74-6d19-51d7-b6b4-c00f66ac9476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\text{ feet}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392647} {:answer/id #uuid "71d4db78-dc17-5a6a-ab36-7c6870d8b79b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.5\\text{ feet}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb340c74-6d19-51d7-b6b4-c00f66ac9476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\text{ feet}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392647} :answer-field/id #uuid "50391d24-edeb-556b-9cad-499093215c2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392643}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7963534b-05e1-5074-b217-dce61952496a" :question/math-academy-id "q-48381" :question/problem "The sides (in feet) of a square are increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the square is numerically equal to four times the rate of increase of the diagonals. What is the length of the diagonals of the square at that moment?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=4\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$, so replacing $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =h\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 4\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} & =h\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 4 & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at that particular moment, we have that the length of the diagonals of the square is $4\\text{ feet}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373188 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064373 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2edbb40a-1d59-5c75-bcc0-93584ec9c030" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for the Rate of Change of a Side Length Given the Rate of Change of the Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0637a4d0-6b43-5e08-9f4b-cf24e433081c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373190} {:answer/id #uuid "5c81e1aa-915b-5422-99f0-467b0e070289" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373191} {:answer/id #uuid "815fc749-b444-53da-9fc2-2e5989eb4ba9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373192} {:answer/id #uuid "b8909279-d59c-52c0-9000-cf5c167053c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373193} {:answer/id #uuid "696bc3f8-d7cc-5732-ae41-e24bf7b3365b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c81e1aa-915b-5422-99f0-467b0e070289" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373191} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8b8ebc5-b1cb-51e5-ace6-4230ff06b936" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6f517ab-ab71-54a6-88e9-bc765cc4550f" :question/math-academy-id "q-73318" :question/problem "The area of a square decreases at a rate of $12\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$ If $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square, respectively, then find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$, where $t$ is the time in minutes." :question/worked-solution "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate, and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/f4/f4125cebd7553bb8b106a8baff39a6e4a120b9d9e5cdf2c4c9c0a9a676558897.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-12\\text{ in}{}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{6}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064373 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2edbb40a-1d59-5c75-bcc0-93584ec9c030" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for the Rate of Change of a Side Length Given the Rate of Change of the Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ae14c7f-afe1-5af8-a226-578eb25a1d6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520781} {:answer/id #uuid "4a639175-1465-5a64-b32f-cf91e2f5edec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520933} {:answer/id #uuid "a656bd00-67bb-5523-830c-b4b23913621b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521137} {:answer/id #uuid "d75272db-a98d-5628-8afb-0b5d34f9ad85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521246} {:answer/id #uuid "ea6596e5-4bc0-5c9f-8401-1fc0a34be568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a639175-1465-5a64-b32f-cf91e2f5edec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520933} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f4af299-5b93-51eb-b385-3f0f4ad159a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9a2565d5-0f88-53cc-81b1-7e0ad2dcfafe" :question/math-academy-id "q-73320" :question/problem "The area of a square increases at a rate of $24{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/hour}.$ If $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square respectively, then find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$, where $t$ is the time in hours." :question/worked-solution "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate,  and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/d7/d713fe1a9286ec36d413bda32cce5ea4327003e6cc4f8a40a45cb945166d68c1.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =24\\text{ m}{}^{2}\\text{/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{12}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064374 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e2af960-841b-56a9-bc7c-099bf82e0fe8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Area Given a Rate of Change of a Diagonal: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "059a746a-7219-54b6-8c45-7f39d3a7b05a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "93\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520771} {:answer/id #uuid "2f35a4a7-d9a4-52b8-80c9-e437acc5e057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22.5\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520862} {:answer/id #uuid "6b377338-8877-5e4e-8347-0093dfa4516c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "47\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521007} {:answer/id #uuid "93b930d8-b345-570b-a7c7-dc0aff86eabe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521101} {:answer/id #uuid "ae39ea77-3301-5cbf-96b5-205a58c99d1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae39ea77-3301-5cbf-96b5-205a58c99d1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521153} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a87e9bd-e6be-51a6-8fce-13d2940093e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c95a9e41-829a-5344-af50-15a5606363d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-73369" :question/problem "![](images/8b/8b4196d82bf27236e6c28651a01678c60c41bc851afbb7aad2e754578ba330e9.png)\n\nJacob is a carpenter. He wishes to reduce the size of a wooden square while maintaining its shape. The lengths of the diagonals of the square decrease at a rate of $2\\text{ cm}\\text{/h}.$ At what rate, in squared centimeters per hour, is the area of ​​the square decreasing when the length of the diagonal is $45\\,\\text{cm}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ decreases at a rate of $2\\,\\text{cm/h},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=45.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-2\\cdot 45=-90\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the square is decreasing at a rate of $90\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/h}.$"}]}
